# ─── REQUIRED — the app does not boot without these ────────────────────────────
# Copy this file to `.env` and fill them in. NEVER commit `.env` (it is
# gitignored) and never paste real connection strings back into this template.
#
# MIRROR_PRODUCTION_DB / MIRROR_DEV_DB — MAIN read mirrors.
# DATABASE_URL / DEV_DATABASE_URL — MAIN primaries used only by explicit
#                      application mutation workflows.
# ADMIN_DATABASE_URL — Admin-panel Postgres (admin_users, audit events, creator
#                      deals, expenses). Full read/write. Keep this on the
#                      DIRECT endpoint for introspection and reviewed SQL
#                      migrations.
# SESSION_SECRET     — JWT signing key for admin sessions (`jose`). Use a long
#                      random value: `openssl rand -hex 32`.
DATABASE_URL=
MIRROR_PRODUCTION_DB=
MIRROR_DEV_DB=
# Dedicated read-only source for complete historical creator usages/sessions.
CREATOR_ANALYTICS_DATABASE_URL=
ADMIN_DATABASE_URL=
SESSION_SECRET=

# Password for the seeded initial admin (`npm run admin:seed`). The seed refuses
# to run when this is unset and never logs the value.
# ADMIN_SEED_PASSWORD=

# Optional MAIN-DB dev target. When set, the in-app prod/dev toggle
# (`admin_db_env` cookie) can point reads at a dev game DB instead of prod —
# see the Drizzle environment resolver in src/lib/db.ts.
# Read-only dev traffic uses MIRROR_DEV_DB. Writes use DEV_DATABASE_URL.
# DEV_DATABASE_URL=

# Dedicated real development database used only by the private /eos battle-test
# user picker. This client is server-only and forces every session read-only.
# BATTLE_TEST_DEV_DATABASE_URL=

# ─── Database connection pooler (optional) — SERVER-ONLY ───────────────────────
# The runtime uses node-postgres through Drizzle, so a managed pooler
# (PgBouncer / Neon / Supabase / Supavisor) is a drop-in. Leave the *_POOLED
# vars blank to connect directly via DATABASE_URL / ADMIN_DATABASE_URL; set
# them to route runtime traffic through the pooler with a safe direct fallback.
# Keep ADMIN_DATABASE_URL on the direct endpoint for introspection and SQL
# migrations; ADMIN_DATABASE_URL_POOLED only routes runtime traffic.
# See docs/DB_POOLER_DEPLOYMENT.md for the vendor-side checklist.
# DATABASE_URL_POOLED=
# ADMIN_DATABASE_URL_POOLED=

# ─── Upstash Redis (shared cache + rate limiting) — SERVER-ONLY ────────────────
# Optional shared, cross-instance cache + rate limiter (see src/lib/cache/redis.ts).
# DORMANT BY DEFAULT: leave blank and every cache/rate-limit call degrades to a
# pure pass-through (limiter fails open, cache computes fresh) — behavior is
# identical to not having it. The Vercel "Upstash for Redis" Marketplace
# integration injects the KV_REST_API_* names; a native Upstash setup uses the
# UPSTASH_REDIS_REST_* names. Provide either pair (URL + token). Never commit
# real values. Do NOT use REDIS_URL/KV_URL (TCP) — the SDK speaks the REST API.
KV_REST_API_URL=
KV_REST_API_TOKEN=
# UPSTASH_REDIS_REST_URL=
# UPSTASH_REDIS_REST_TOKEN=

# ─── Sentry (error + performance monitoring) ───────────────────────────────────
# DORMANT BY DEFAULT: with no DSN the SDK is disabled (sends nothing) and the
# build is byte-identical (see src/instrumentation*.ts + next.config.ts). Set
# the DSNs to enable runtime capture; set ORG/PROJECT/AUTH_TOKEN to also upload
# source maps + tag releases during the build. Never commit real values.
SENTRY_DSN=
NEXT_PUBLIC_SENTRY_DSN=
# Sampling (0..1). Defaults to 0.1 when unset.
# SENTRY_TRACES_SAMPLE_RATE=0.1
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SENTRY_TRACES_SAMPLE_RATE=0.1
# Error-only Session Replay (normal sessions always remain disabled). Text and
# inputs are masked and media is blocked. Defaults to 1; set 0 to opt out.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SENTRY_REPLAYS_ON_ERROR_SAMPLE_RATE=1
# Build-time source-map upload (CI / Vercel only):
# SENTRY_ORG=
# SENTRY_PROJECT=
# SENTRY_AUTH_TOKEN=

# ─── Passkeys / WebAuthn (admin 2FA second factor) — SERVER-ONLY ───────────────
# Passkeys are an additive ALTERNATIVE to the TOTP code at /verify-2fa (see
# src/lib/webauthn.ts). OPTIONAL: leave both blank and the Relying-Party ID +
# origin are derived from the incoming request host, which works when the admin
# is served from a single canonical domain. Set them to PIN the RP ID/origin in
# production (REQUIRED once served from multiple hosts). WEBAUTHN_RP_ID is the
# bare registrable domain (no scheme/port); WEBAUTHN_ORIGIN is a COMMA-SEPARATED
# list of full origins.
#
# MULTI-HOST: a passkey is bound to its RP ID and the browser only offers it on
# that domain or a registrable PARENT of it — so with the dashboard on four
# packydash.com hosts the RP ID must be the shared parent, `packydash.com`.
# Pinning a sub-domain would strand every passkey registered on the others. The
# origin check is separate and must match exactly, hence the list.
# WEBAUTHN_RP_ID=packydash.com
# WEBAUTHN_ORIGIN=https://packydash.com,https://packs.packydash.com,https://fraud.packydash.com,https://marketing.packydash.com

# ─── Code pepper (promo codes + gift cards) — SERVER-ONLY, PER-ENVIRONMENT ─────
# A stored `code_hash` is sha256(NORMALISED_CODE + pepper). The BACKEND
# recomputes that hash with its OWN pepper to find the row at redeem time, so a
# code the admin writes is only redeemable if both sides used the SAME secret.
#
# Named after gift cards for historical reasons only — the backend hashes gift
# cards and PROMO CODES with one shared secret (`GIFT_CARD_PEPPER` in its own
# env, min 32 chars, see packy-backend src/config/env.ts). It does not decide
# which kind of code you create; the table you insert into does.
#
# Each backend deployment has its OWN value, so the admin — which talks to both
# environments via the header env toggle — needs one per environment. Using
# prod's pepper against the dev database mints codes that look perfect and
# redeem as "Code not found" (this happened, 2026-07-23). Both the reward
# campaign composer and /promo-codes now resolve this by env and THROW when the
# one they need is missing, rather than minting unredeemable codes.
#
# GIFT_CARD_PEPPER      = the PROD backend's GIFT_CARD_PEPPER
# GIFT_CARD_PEPPER_DEV  = the DEV backend's GIFT_CARD_PEPPER
# GIFT_CARD_PEPPER=
# GIFT_CARD_PEPPER_DEV=

# ─── Antifraud workspace — ALL OPTIONAL, SERVER-ONLY unless marked ─────────────
# The Antifraud sub-app (/antifraud, and later fraud.packydash.com) runs
# ENTIRELY off the admin DB with none of these set: cases and in-app
# notifications work. Each variable below switches on one extra
# integration, and every one of them fails CLOSED when absent (never open).
#
# Optional external staff delivery. In-app notifications work without these.
# ANTIFRAUD_DISCORD_WEBHOOK_URL=
# ANTIFRAUD_TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN=
#
# Dedicated monitor service. These values are SERVER-ONLY and must never use a
# NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix. The browser only talks to authenticated same-origin
# dashboard bridges.
#   ANTIFRAUD_MONITOR_API_URL   - HTTPS service base URL.
#   ANTIFRAUD_MONITOR_API_TOKEN - read/ticket token; never the admin write token.
#   ANTIFRAUD_MONITOR_API_ADMIN_TOKEN - privileged review and rule write token.
# ANTIFRAUD_MONITOR_API_URL=
# ANTIFRAUD_MONITOR_API_TOKEN=
# ANTIFRAUD_MONITOR_API_ADMIN_TOKEN=
#
# Durable signal-ingest integration:
#   ANTIFRAUD_INGEST_SECRET — shared HMAC secret for POST /api/antifraud/ingest,
#                            the DURABLE path (signals persist + open cases +
#                            ping on-call even with nobody watching). Signature
#                            is sha256 HMAC over `${timestamp}.${rawBody}`, same
#                            construction as the rewards-bot webhook. UNSET =
#                            the endpoint refuses everything with 503.
# ANTIFRAUD_INGEST_SECRET=
# The same secret authenticates /api/antifraud/discord-events. Events are
# queued for the bot only in this exact admin guild.
# ADMIN_GUILD_ID=1483064422778798112
#
# Absolute base used to build clickable links inside Discord/Telegram pings.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_ANTIFRAUD_URL=https://fraud.packydash.com

# ─── Multi-host routing (src/lib/app-hosts.ts) ────────────────────────────────
# The dashboard answers on four hostnames from ONE deployment:
#   packydash.com            → main admin dashboard  (lands /dashboard)
#   packs.packydash.com      → Pack Studio           (lands /pack-studio)
#   fraud.packydash.com      → Antifraud workspace   (lands /antifraud)
#   marketing.packydash.com  → marketing surfaces    (lands /analytics)
# All four are hard-coded in app-hosts.ts — no env needed for the real ones.
#
# Add EXTRA hosts (preview domains, or `packs.localhost:3000` for local work)
# with a comma-separated `host=basePath` map. `/` or an empty base = a landing
# host (no rewrite). NEXT_PUBLIC_ = exposed to the browser, nothing secret here.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_HOST_MAP=fraud.localhost=/antifraud,packs.localhost=/pack-studio
#
# ─── Cross-subdomain session (optional) ────────────────────────────────────────
# Setting a cookie DOMAIN makes ONE login valid on the apex and every sub-domain
# — needed so switching to fraud.packydash.com doesn't ask for credentials
# again. UNSET (the default) keeps the session cookie host-only, exactly as it
# behaves today. Only ever widen this to a domain you own entirely: every host
# under it can read the cookie.
# SESSION_COOKIE_DOMAIN=.packydash.com
WHOP_COMPANY_ID=
